--
--
Instantiating a function template whose (unnamed) parameter type is a
type-yielding builtin, `template <typename _Tp> void
probe(__add_rvalue_reference(_Tp));`, by merely NAMING it
(`probe<int> && true`) reports `symbol '__add_rvalue_reference' is
unknown`: during instantiation the parameter's builtin type node
degrades to a plain name lookup instead of the ID_add_rvalue_reference
type route (cpp_typecheck_type.cpp handles the node when it survives
as a type).  This is the ROOT of gcc16_optional_transform's
`_M_payload is unknown`: _Optional_payload's defaulted bool NTTP is
`is_trivially_move_assignable_v<_Tp> && ...` where that _v helper is
exactly this shape, so evaluating the default fails, the
specialization choice fails, and the payload member never materializes.
VALIDITY: gcc-16-only builtin; certified by g++ 16.2 in the gcc16-gate
docker image (tumbleweed).
Derived from the cvise-reduced gcc16_optional_transform artifact
(round 106 manual backward delta, /tmp/k106 h1-h4; the load-bearing
chain was established by 14 checked deltas: a0->a5->e2->e4->f1->h1,
with f2 proving a PORTABLE `_Tp&&` parameter works).
